{{block center|<math>\frac{\text{W}\epsilon^2}{g}\cdot\frac{d^2\theta}{dt^2}+\text{K}_1\frac{d\theta}{dt}+\text{K}_2\left(\frac{d\theta}{dt}\right)^2+\text{W}\ .\ m\ .\ \theta=0.</math>}}
If <math>\text{K}_2</math> is zero, a complete solution is—
{{block center|<math>\theta=\text{A}_e^{\frac{-\text{K}_1g}{2\text{W}\epsilon^2}t}\sin\left(t\sqrt{\frac{mg}{\epsilon^2}-\frac{\text{K}_1{}^2g^2}{4\text{W}^2\epsilon^4}}+\beta\right),</math> . . (7)}}
where A and B are arbitrary, and the period <math>\text{T}r</math> of resisted rolling is given by
{{block center|<math>\text{T}_r=\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{\frac{mg}{\epsilon^2}-\frac{\text{K}_1{}^2g^2}{4\text{W}^2\epsilon^4}}}=\frac{\text{T}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{\text{K}_1{}^2g}{4\text{W}^2\epsilon^2m}}}.</math>}}
It appears, therefore, that the period is slightly increased and the amplitude progressively diminished by the resistance. In actual cases where <math>\text{K}_2</math> is necessarily included in the differential equation, the complete solution cannot be conveniently expressed analytically, but it can be determined in effect either by any method of approximate quadrature or by a process of “graphic integration.” The diminution of amplitude can also be approximately obtained by

readings.c2s4:
assuming the motion to be simple harmonic with amplitude <math>\Theta</math> and by equating the work done by the resistances during the roll to the loss of dynamical stability—<math>\text{W}.\ m.\ \Theta\times</math>decrement. The differential equation for the curve of extinction is thus obtained, and is—
{{block center|<math>-\frac{d\Theta}{dn} = a\,.\,\Theta + b\,.\,\Theta^2,</math>}}
where <math>\Theta =</math> extreme angle (in degrees) reached at any particular oscillation, <math>n</math> the number of oscillations, and <math>a</math> and <math>b</math> are coefficients equal to
{{block center|<math>\frac{\text{K}_1\pi^2}{2\text{W}m\text{T}}</math> and <math>\frac{4}{3}\cdot\frac{\pi}{180}\,\frac{\text{K}_2\pi^2}{\text{W}m\text{T}^2}</math>}}

{{smaller block|<sup>2</sup> Given by Sir W. H. White, F.R.S., in a paper read before the Institution of Naval Architects in 1895.}}
